<!--
# Custom Domain Handle

Point a custom-domain atproto handle (`alice.example.com`) at a Bluesky
account. The account itself lives on bsky.social and is only named here, as an
`<AtprotoAccount/>`: the ONE piece of infrastructure a custom-domain handle
needs is a DNS TXT record, `_atproto.alice.example.com` holding
`did=did:plc:...`, declared below and published by `deploy`.

## Prerequisites

A domain on a Cloudflare zone, `tofu` on PATH, and `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` in
the process environment (a `secrets.toml` record with `role = "env_var"`).
Nothing else, and less than that for most of the walk: this stack declares no
AWS resource at all,
`<StateBackendToggle/>` keeps the tofu state under `.beet/infra` unless
`--s3-backend` is passed, and the stack opts out of state encryption
(`state_passphrase={None}`, its state holds nothing secret) so there is no
passphrase. Only the apply inside `deploy` authenticates, so `validate` and
`plan` run on `tofu` alone.

## Walkthrough

1. Create the account at <https://bsky.social> (or in the app) under any
   temporary handle, and verify its email. Manual, once.
2. Find its did. Settings -> Account shows it, or ask the same resolver the
   probe below asks:
   `https://public.api.bsky.app/xrpc/com.atproto.identity.resolveHandle?handle=<temp>.bsky.social`
3. Declare it: replace `example.com` and the two `<AtprotoAccount/>`s below
   with the domain you own and the handles and dids from step 2. An account
   whose handle IS the domain is the APEX handle, ie `example.com` itself
   rather than a label under it, which is usually what an organisation's own
   account wants; drop it from `accounts` if you only want the subdomain.
4. Run it through the beet binary built with the handle block:

       cargo run -p beet-cli --features infra,extra,atproto -- --entry=examples/infra/custom_handle_domain.bsx --stage=prod validate
       cargo run -p beet-cli --features infra,extra,atproto -- --entry=examples/infra/custom_handle_domain.bsx --stage=prod plan
       cargo run -p beet-cli --features infra,extra,atproto -- --entry=examples/infra/custom_handle_domain.bsx --stage=prod deploy

   `deploy` ends in `<AtprotoHandleProbe/>`, so it passes only once the handle
   actually resolves to the declared did through Bluesky's own resolver, which
   is the same walk the app makes. DNS takes a moment: a probe that fails
   immediately after the apply usually just needs re-running (`probe`).
5. Claim it in the app: Settings -> Handle -> "I have my own domain" ->
   `alice.example.com` (or the bare `example.com` for the apex) -> Verify DNS
   Record. Manual, once. The temporary handle is released back to bsky.social.

`--stage=prod` is not decoration, see the `dns_stage` note on the block below.
Replace the zone id on the stack with your zone's, from its Cloudflare
dashboard url: it is an identifier, not a secret.

## Teardown

Remove the handle from the declaration and re-run `deploy`; the record retires
with it. Switch the account back to a `*.bsky.social` handle in the app FIRST,
or its sessions keep working while its handle displays as invalid.

## Verification

Bluesky's resolvers cache a FAILED resolution too, per replica and for up to an
hour, long past the record's TTL. So a claim attempted before the record was
right keeps failing with "Failed to verify handle" after the record is fixed,
while `dig` and the probe already agree. Wait it out rather than redeploying:
nothing on this side is stale.

## One entry owns a zone

`<ZoneAudit/>` asserts that its own stack's declarations are exactly what the
zone holds, so a handle record published from here reads as a stray to any
other beet entry auditing the same zone: that entry's `audit` reports it, and
its `audit --fix` DELETES it. If your zone already belongs to another entry (a
mail stack, say), declare the `<AtprotoHandleBlock/>` inside THAT entry's stack
beside its other tags rather than copying this one, or add the `_atproto.*`
names to its audit allowlist with a reason. The block is its own tag precisely
so it can be authored wherever the zone's owner is.
-->
<CliServer {CallOnReady}>
<Router {(RequestLogger, HelpHandler)}>
	<RequireCfg cfg="feature:infra && feature:extra && feature:atproto"/>
	<PackageConfig title="Custom Domain Handle" app_name="atproto-handles"
		description="Publish the _atproto records that make a domain you own an atproto handle"/>
	<!-- the accounts, each declared once and named by `bx:ref` from the block
	     below. The dids are placeholders in the right shape: replace them with
	     the ones from step 2. -->
	<AtprotoAccount bx:ref="org" handle="example.com"
		did="did:plc:aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa" display_name="Example"/>
	<AtprotoAccount bx:ref="alice" handle="alice.example.com"
		did="did:plc:bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb" display_name="Alice"/>
	<!-- the state backend is a property of the LAUNCH, not of a stack's identity,
	     so the toggle sits outside the stack and lands on the process `Deployment`.
	     Local by default, which is what keeps this stack Cloudflare-only. -->
	<StateBackendToggle/>
	<!-- the zone the records land in, an address resolved by ancestry, and
	     the region: nothing is deployed into it, it is the AWS provider config
	     every stack renders with, declared because a render refuses to guess
	     one. -->
	<Stack app_name="atproto-handles" state_passphrase={None} {(AwsRegion("us-west-2"), CloudflareZone{domain:"example.com", id:"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef"})}>
		<!-- 💡 what a deploy DOES, as an ordered group rather than a route, and
		     what a teardown does, authored in the same CONVERGENCE order and run
		     backwards. This stack owns nothing outside terraform, so its
		     teardown is `tofu destroy` plus the state carriers a deploy needed
		     before it. -->
		<Group bx:ref="deploy_up">
			<TofuApply/>
			<AtprotoHandleProbe/>
		</Group>
		<Group bx:ref="deploy_down">
			<StackTeardown/>
			<TofuDestroy/>
		</Group>

		<!-- the standard IaC verbs (deploy / destroy / validate / plan / ... ),
		     resolving this <Stack> by ancestry and running the two groups
		     above. -->
		<DeployRoutes deploy={$deploy_up} destroy={$deploy_down}/>

		<!-- 💡 the whole declaration. One TXT record per account, at
		     `_atproto.<handle>`, holding `did=<did>`.

		     The first account's handle is the domain, which is the APEX: its
		     record is `_atproto.example.com`.
		     Note that this is still a name UNDER the apex, not a record AT it,
		     so an apex handle costs the zone's apex nothing.

		     `dns_stage="prod"` because a handle record is a REAL global name,
		     exactly like a mail name: `_atproto.alice.example.com` is the only
		     record that makes that handle work, so a second stage deploying this
		     same declaration would publish a second TXT record at it and the
		     handle would stop resolving deterministically. Outside `prod` this
		     block therefore publishes nothing at all, which is why every command
		     above passes `--stage=prod`. The records land in the
		     `CloudflareZone` declared on the stack. -->
		<AtprotoHandleBlock domain="example.com" dns_stage="prod"
			accounts={[$org, $alice]}/>

		<!-- the probe on its own: cheap, read-only and the honest answer to "is
		     the handle still wired up", which nothing about a converged record
		     can tell you. -->
		<Route path="probe" {ExchangeSequence}>
			<AtprotoHandleProbe/>
		</Route>

		<!-- 💡 the zone holds exactly what this stack declares, plus what it was
		     told to expect. Everything allowed below is a decision with a reason;
		     a zone serving a website has at least these, and they are nothing to
		     do with handles. Run with `--fix` to delete the strays rather than
		     report them, and read the header's ownership note first. -->
		<Route path="audit" {ExchangeSequence}>
			<ZoneAudit allowed={[
				{name:"example.com", record_type:"A",
					reason:"the apex website, owned by whoever deploys the site"},
				{name:"www.example.com",
					reason:"the www hostname, same owner as the apex"},
				{name:"example.com", record_type:"MX",
					reason:"the domain's mail, owned by its mail provider"},
				{name:"example.com", record_type:"TXT",
					reason:"the apex SPF and any domain-ownership verification"},
			]}/>
		</Route>
	</Stack>
</Router>
</CliServer>
